![](/images/articles/save-money-2.jpeg)

[Last time I gave you eight reasons why leading with a "save money" message in enterprise software is risky](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/perils-save-money-message-part-1-steve-bamberger-2iamc). Today we'll talk about some exceptions to that rule, and how to leverage the value of cost-cutting even when those exceptions don't apply.

There are times when saving money can be your lead message:

1.  Massive scale. If your customer has high enough volumes, small improvements can create enormous benefits. If you can save Amazon two cents on each transaction, go for it. In the real world, most enterprise software investments have human-scale benefits, meaningful risks, and longer payback periods.
2.  Hard costs savings. You can pitch costs savings when your TCO is meaningfully less than the incumbent. For example, if you propose a SaaS solution to replace an on-prem product, and your solution costs meaningfully less to own and operate than the existing system, you may be able to lead with lower costs. But be careful — this argument assumes there's little downside (for example, you provide the same important capabilities as the incumbent) and little risk (for example, the risk of switching).
3.  Commodity pricing. In a commodity pricing situation, you're in a race to the bottom on cost already. Commodity tech is not the norm in enterprise software, but it happens. Again, beware — there's often a perceived customer benefit to maintaining stability and uniformity in the installed base even when there's a meaningfully cheaper option. (Ask anyone who has tried to sell a Postgres database to replace Oracle.)

So now what? I've argued that using cost savings as a lead message isn't great, and these exceptions don't apply to you. How can you leverage cost savings as a value proposition without falling into these traps?

  

[Simply being cheapER is a loser message](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/tyranny-er-steve-bamberger). But cost savings is an outstanding *supplementary* message when it's anchored by a *compelling, business-essential, urgent priority.* The formula is this:

  - Lead with the unique value that ONLY YOU can provide to solve an urgent, business-essential problem. This is where you want your champion focused.
  - Supplement with cost savings to reinforce that there are no economically viable alternatives to your solution, and (when you're ready for it) your business case. This can help get the CFO on board as an approver for your program.

Let's look at some examples:

1.  You are looking to *double your business*. But you can't possibly hire qualified people fast enough to achieve that goal. ONLY ACME allows you to scale your business fast enough to meet your growth objectives. (AND the system will scale far more economically than your current approach, saving you money in the process.)
2.  You are looking to *reduce attrition among high value employees*. But you don't know how to do that in a targeted way. ONLY ACME provides meaningful, actionable insights that informs interventions that reduce turnover. (AND it will pay for itself in the cost of attrition, and it's cheaper than building this system on your own with your existing toolsets.)
3.  You are looking to *launch a new product*. But your current billing system will take too long to adapt to your new pricing model. ONLY ACME will allow you to get to market on time. (AND it will be at a far lower cost than trying to adapt your current systems.)

Follow this formula, and your cost savings message will amplify your success rather than hold you back.
